WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.900
Welcome to This Explainer. Today, we're taking on a massive challenge that honestly plagues almost every product and development team out there. We're diving into source material that can transform your product backlog from absolute, utter chaos into crystal clarity. So, whether you're a product manager, a scrum master, or a developer, let's explore exactly how backlog mechanics should work. We're going to extract the exact strategies you need to make your backlog a genuinely powerful engine for delivery.

2
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.460
Be honest for a second.

3
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.440
Think about it.

4
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.240
Is your product backlog a dynamic, strategic roadmap?

5
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.080
Or is it a graveyard where good ideas just go to die?

6
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.540
We've all seen it, right?

7
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.580
The nightmare scenario of a 500-item, completely chaotic backlog

8
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.680
where absolutely nobody knows what's important

9
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.960
and somehow everything is a top priority.

10
00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.540
Well, today we're contrasting that overwhelming mess with the ideal,

11
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.900
a lean, actionable, and rigorously prioritized roadmap

12
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.040
that your whole team actually understands.

13
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.200
Okay, let's dive into this.

14
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.100
We'll start big with the iceberg rules of backlog management,

15
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.920
then zoom in on writing winning user stories and defining acceptance criteria.

16
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.740
After that, we'll tackle time estimates and story points.

17
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:10.020
And finally, we'll wrap up with some advanced prioritization strategies.

18
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.880
First up, section one, the iceberg rules for backlog management.

19
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.060
A fundamental truth, straight from the Scrum Guide,

20
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.260
is that a product backlog is a living, breathing document.

21
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.120
But actually, a lot of teams mix up two really critical artifacts here.

22
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.020
So let's clear it up.

23
00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.440
The product backlog, that's owned entirely by the product manager.

24
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.840
It spans the entire lifetime of the product.

25
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.180
It's highly dynamic, and it contains items with totally varying levels of detail.

26
00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.160
But the sprint backlog is different.

27
00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.640
That is owned strictly by the development team.

28
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.740
It spans just one single sprint.

29
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.060
The scope is absolutely locked in, and every single item in there is highly detailed.

30
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.220
Blurring the lines between those two is basically step one toward agile chaos.

31
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.140
The source material suggests managing the product backlog like a giant iceberg.

32
00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.740
The level of detail should scale directly with how close you are to actual development.

33
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:09.080
So the top 20% of your backlog, the very tip of the iceberg, should be highly detailed, estimated, and completely ready to go for sprint planning.

34
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.740
The middle 30% has moderate detail.

35
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:15.220
You know, you understand the general idea, but it definitely needs some refinement.

36
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:17.500
And that massive bottom 50%?

37
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:20.820
Those should just be vague ideas, epics, and titles.

38
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:26.480
No deep details allowed whatsoever. Agile expert Roman Pitchler puts this perfectly. He says,

39
00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:30.760
don't try to write detailed user stories for everything up front. You'll waste time on stories

40
00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.260
that will change or never get built. Detail only what's coming next. Honestly, trying to write out

41
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.560
exhaustive specifications for the bottom 50% of your iceberg is a massive waste of your time.

42
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.460
Because let's face it, by the time you actually get around to building them, the market or your

43
00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:49.220
product itself will have completely changed. Moving right along to section two, writing winning user

44
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:55.260
stories. Okay, now that we understand the macro view, let's zoom in a bit. First, we really need

45
00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:01.780
to understand scale. An epic takes weeks or even months to complete. A user story takes days. Both

46
00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:06.980
of those are written by the product manager. But tasks? Tasks take hours, and they are written by

47
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:12.400
the developers. A huge, super common anti-pattern is product managers trying to write technical

48
00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:18.920
tasks, like build an API endpoint. Look, the PM's job is to define the what and the why. For example,

49
00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:24.260
as a manager, I want to export a weekly PDF report. It is totally up to the developers to

50
00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:29.960
define the how through specific technical tasks. To really pressure test those stories before they

51
00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:34.680
ever reach the development team, the sources highlight Bill Wake's famous invest framework.

52
00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:40.140
It's a great checklist. Your story must be independent, so story B doesn't rely entirely

53
00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:44.420
on story A just to function. It's got to be negotiable, right? Not some rigid contract.

54
00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.900
It needs to be valuable to the user or the business, estimable by the team, small enough

55
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.780
to actually fit into a single sprint, and finally testable.

56
00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.520
Just saying the system will be fast isn't testable.

57
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.400
Saying the report loads in under two seconds, now that is testable.

58
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:00.800
Section 3.

59
00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.680
Defining Success with Acceptance Criteria

60
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:09.020
So how do we shift from the general narrative of a user story into the strict, hard boundaries

61
00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:10.240
of its actual scope?

62
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.020
The answer is Acceptance Criteria, or AC.

63
00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:18.220
These are the specific conditions that absolutely must be met for a user story to be considered finished.

64
00:04:18.620 --> 00:04:23.600
They define exactly what is in scope and, maybe more importantly, what is out of scope.

65
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.080
Without clear acceptance criteria, you're essentially forcing your developers to guess what you want.

66
00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.500
And let me tell you, mind reading is not a reliable development strategy.

67
00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:34.680
It literally always leads to disappointment at the end of the sprint.

68
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:40.300
The absolute best way to write AC is using the Behavior-Driven Development, or BDD, format.

69
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.700
It's just a super simple three-step logic.

70
00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.720
One, given a start condition.

71
00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.020
Two, when an action occurs.

72
00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:49.620
Three, then the expected result happens.

73
00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.520
So, for example, given that I am on the reports page and there is data,

74
00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.640
when I click export PDF, then a formatted PDF file downloads to my machine.

75
00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.500
See, this simple little formula just entirely eliminates ambiguity.

76
00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.880
But hey, even with the best formatting in the world,

77
00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.800
we have to remember Jeff Patton's legendary rule.

78
00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:11.900
A user story is a placeholder for a conversation.

79
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.560
Perfect documentation can never, ever replace actual human collaboration.

80
00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:19.740
The ticket isn't the final product. It's an invitation.

81
00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:24.140
An invitation for the product manager and the dev team to talk, clarify things,

82
00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.380
and get fully aligned before a single line of code is ever written.

83
00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.980
Section 4. Time Estimates and Story Points

84
00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:35.580
This alignment we just talked about, it usually happens during backlog refinement.

85
00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:41.460
And the crucial takeaway here is understanding exactly why teams use story points instead of hours.

86
00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:46.820
Story points measure relative complexity, effort, and uncertainty, and they're assessed by team

87
00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:52.160
consensus. Hours, on the other hand, measure expected time, usually just guessed by a single

88
00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:57.500
developer. If you try to convert story points into hours, you know, saying, okay, one point equals

89
00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:02.060
one day, you're completely destroying the collective knowledge, the uncertainty padding,

90
00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:07.120
and the risk assessment benefits of agile estimation. Just never equate points directly

91
00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:13.020
to ours. To avoid that trap of false precision, the research points to using t-shirt sizing map

92
00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:19.840
to the Fibonacci sequence. 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13. By using steps that grow further and further apart,

93
00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:24.240
you literally force the team to acknowledge uncertainty. An extra small ticket might be a

94
00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:29.280
simple text change worth one point, but a large ticket? That might be a super complex external API

95
00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.720
integration worth 8 or 13 points. And honestly, anything larger than an XL, say 20 points,

96
00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.080
is actually just an epic in disguise, and it needs to be broken down.

97
00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:40.460
Oh, and using techniques like planning poker,

98
00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.200
where everyone reveals their estimates simultaneously, that's a game changer.

99
00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:47.540
It helps overcome anchoring bias and stops the loudest person in the room

100
00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:48.860
from dictating the whole estimate.

101
00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:54.320
And finally, Section 5, Advanced Prioritization and Debt Management.

102
00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.660
So, now that your tickets are refined and estimated,

103
00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.160
how do you mathematically choose what to build next?

104
00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.140
Enter WSJF, or Weighted Shortest Job First.

105
00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:07.140
It's a really powerful prioritization formula.

106
00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:11.380
You calculate it by taking the cost of delay and dividing it by the job duration, which

107
00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:14.200
by the way is basically just your story point.

108
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:18.860
Cost of delay itself is calculated by adding up user value, time criticality, and risk

109
00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:19.860
reduction.

110
00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:24.140
It forces you to look at both the massive value of a feature and how quickly you can actually

111
00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.140
get it out the door.

112
00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:29.820
Let's think about how this math works in a real-world scenario, by comparing two totally

113
00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:30.960
different features.

114
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.180
On one hand, we have a highly visible user feature, a PDF export.

115
00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:41.460
It has a cost of delay of 13 and a duration of 3, giving it a WSJF score of 4.3.

116
00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.860
But compare that to a back-end security upgrade.

117
00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:47.620
The user won't even see it, but its time criticality and risk reduction are absolutely

118
00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:50.640
huge, giving it a cost of delay of 31.

119
00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.500
Divided by a duration of 5, its WSJF score comes out to 6.2.

120
00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:59.860
This math brilliantly proves exactly why a boring back-end security upgrade can and

121
00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:05.360
should win priority over a shiny new user feature. It simply delivers the most critical value in the

122
00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:09.120
shortest amount of time. And as you're prioritizing all this, you really need to keep in mind the

123
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.220
concept of dual-track agile. Basically, you actually have two tracks running simultaneously.

124
00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:17.460
The discovery backlog is for validating ideas. The goal here is purely learning,

125
00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:21.780
driven by the PM and the designer. Then, the delivery backlog is for actually building those

126
00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.460
validated stories. Product discovery expert Teresa Torres points out a vital, absolute truth here.

127
00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:30.880
Teams that skip the discovery track and just pull raw ideas straight into delivery are building

128
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:35.640
features based on highly, highly dangerous assumptions. Which brings us to a final

129
00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.500
provocative challenge from the sources. I want you to think about your own backlog right now.

130
00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:45.480
When was the last time you just declared backlog bankruptcy? Seriously, if you have 200 items

131
00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.580
sitting at the bottom of your iceberg that haven't been touched in over six months,

132
00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:54.340
the experts say delete them, archive them, poof. I know it sounds terrifying, but if an idea is

133
00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:58.920
truly critical to your product success, it will inevitably come back up. So my question for you is,

134
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.140
are you brave enough to delete the clutter and finally turn your chaos into clarity?

